`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `GameDefinition<FloeDebugApi>`: the game instance
class, a level registry holding the single level the game runs in, and
`startLevel` naming it. The game opens that one level and never opens another;
every screen is a value of the state's `screen` field, so the world and its game
state live for the whole session and a level advance in Floe's own sense
rearranges the strait rather than opening a world. The instance's `initialize`
registers the actions, defines the cues, and returns the debug surface. The
level's game mode runs the screens and the rules, and it adds a single player in
its `beginPlay`, possessing nothing; that player's controller is where the
actions are read. The mode's `gameStateClass` is `FloeState`, so the world's game
state is the state `specs/state.md` declares. The actors, components, and
controllers you write draw that state and drive it.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `FloeState` and the types it is built
from, exactly as `specs/state.md` states them, and `FloeDebugApi`, the debugging
and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

