## Code quality

The code is fit for a long-lived codebase shared with human developers. The
type-check, lint, and format checks pass, and unit tests cover the simulation.

## Coordinate system and presentation

All positions and sizes in this specification are given in the game's own logical
units on a fixed stage of `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` (`1280 x 720`, 16:9). The origin
`(0, 0)` is the top-left; `x` increases to the right and `y` increases downward.
The strait sits inside that stage and its geometry is in `specs/strait.md`. There
is one coordinate system in this game and it is the stage's: every position, in
this specification and in everything the game reports, is measured from the
stage's top-left corner.

- `STAGE_W x STAGE_H` is the game's logical design size. Fitting it to the
  browser window is the runtime's: the uniform scale that preserves the aspect
  ratio, the letterboxed centering, and the device pixel ratio. The complete
  stage is therefore on screen at every window size, on load and at any pixel
  density.
- The letterbox bars around the stage carry the stage's background color.
- Draw in logical units, and take the canvas element's own size from the runtime
  alone.
- Game logic operates in logical units, independent of the rendered scale.

Two conventions apply without exception, because a body and a lane item are
different shapes:

| What | Its reported position is |
| --- | --- |
| The critter, a bear, the bonus catch | Its **center**. |
| A vehicle, a floe | Its **left edge**. It spans `[x, x + 32 * len)` on its row, where `len` is its length in tiles. |

## The fixed timestep

The simulation runs on a fixed timestep of `TICK_HZ` (`120`) steps per second, so
one simulation tick, `TICK_DT`, is exactly `1/120` s. Every rate in this
specification is per second and every duration is in seconds, and both are
integrated in whole ticks of that length.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A frame reports how much time has elapsed and the simulation advances by the
whole `TICK_DT` ticks that elapsed time completes, carrying the remainder into
the next frame, so the number of ticks run over an interval of game time is the
same however that interval was divided into frames.
{{else}}
The engine hands each frame a delta time and the simulation advances by the whole
`TICK_DT` ticks that delta completes, carrying the remainder into the next frame,
so the number of ticks run over an interval of game time is the same however that
interval was divided into frames.
{{/if}}

The tick is the game's whole clock. The lanes' motion, the critter's hop
cooldown, the bear's travel, the crossing timer, the phase pauses, and the bonus
catch's cadence all advance inside it.

## Visual design

The strait is yours to design. Choose the colors and the typography, decide how
the ice, the water, and the shores are drawn, how the bays read, and what the HUD
and each screen look like.

Whatever look you choose, a player reads all of the following at a glance.

| What a player reads | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| The five bands | The near shore, the ice band, the median shelf, the water band, and the far shore are told apart at a glance, and the two solid safe strips read as safe against the two crossing zones on either side. |
| Deep water | A water tile carrying no floe reads as water a player must not stand on, distinct from a floe on the same row. |
| The bays | An open bay reads as an opening in the far shore, distinct from the solid shore beside it, and a filled bay reads as filled. |
| The critter | The critter reads apart from every band it can stand on, and from a floe under it. |
| The bear | A bear reads apart from every band it can travel on. A submerged bear stays trackable: something distinct from the water is drawn where it is, so it is never invisible. |
| The vehicles | A plow, a dogsled, and a car read apart from one another and from the ice under them, and each reads as covering every tile it spans. |
| Text | Every HUD readout and every screen's text is legible against its background at the logical stage size. |

The palette, the type, and every other aspect of the look are yours.
